{
  "dom_id": "step-q342829",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "$$\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=7\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}\\,+$ | $7{e}^{x}\\sqrt{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |",
  "worked_solution": "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAnd the product rule is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}(u\\cdot v)}{\\text{d}x}={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, chain rule, and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={(7\\sqrt{x})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+7\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\text{arsinh}\\,({e}^{x})+7\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{x})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+7\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡{e}^{x}+\\frac{7{e}^{x}\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
